<item>
  <title>Theorems, proofs, and computation</title>
  <dc:creator>Eric Laurence</dc:creator>
  <link>https://eric-laurence.github.io/notes/posts/theorem-demo/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<section id="math-syntax" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="math-syntax">Math syntax</h2>
<p>Inline: the golden ratio <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cvarphi%20=%20%5Ctfrac%7B1+%5Csqrt%7B5%7D%7D%7B2%7D"> satisfies <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cvarphi%5E2%20=%20%5Cvarphi%20+%201">.</p>
<p>Display with alignment:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cbegin%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Cvarphi%5E2%20&amp;=%20%5Cvarphi%20+%201,%20%5C%5C%0A%5Cvarphi%5E3%20&amp;=%20%5Cvarphi%5E2%20+%20%5Cvarphi%20=%202%5Cvarphi%20+%201.%0A%5Cend%7Baligned%7D%0A"></p>
</section>
<section id="theorem-environments" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="theorem-environments">Theorem environments</h2>
<div class="theorem">
<p><strong>Theorem (Fundamental theorem of calculus).</strong> Let <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?f%20:%20%5Ba,b%5D%20%5Cto%20%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D"> be continuous. Then for every <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x%20%5Cin%20(a,b)">, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cfrac%7Bd%7D%7Bdx%7D%5Cint_a%5Ex%20f(t)%5C,dt%20%5C;=%5C;%20f(x).%0A"></p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p><span class="proof-title"><em>Proof</em>. </span>Fix <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x%20%5Cin%20(a,b)">. For <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?h%20%5Cneq%200"> small enough that <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x+h%20%5Cin%20(a,b)">, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bh%7D%5Cint_x%5E%7Bx+h%7D%20f(t)%5C,dt%20%5C;-%5C;%20f(x)%0A%5C;=%5C;%0A%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bh%7D%5Cint_x%5E%7Bx+h%7D%20%5Cbigl(f(t)%20-%20f(x)%5Cbigr)%5C,dt.%0A"> The right-hand side is bounded in absolute value by <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Csup_%7B%7Ct-x%7C%20%5Cle%20%7Ch%7C%7D%20%7Cf(t)-f(x)%7C">, which tends to <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?0"> as <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?h%20%5Cto%200"> by continuity of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?f">.</p>
</div>
<div class="definition">
<p><strong>Definition.</strong> A function <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?f:%20%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D%20%5Cto%20%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D"> is <em>Lipschitz</em> if there exists <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L%20%5Cge%200"> such that <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%7Cf(x)%20-%20f(y)%7C%20%5Cle%20L%7Cx-y%7C"> for all <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x,%20y">.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="computation" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="computation">Computation</h2>
<p>We can run code right inside a post. Here’s <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Csin%20x"> alongside its degree-5 Taylor approximation:</p>
